messages:
Maria Chen: Hey — trying to write a tiny sampleapp for the Go SDK generator/stream API. When I cancel the request ctx mid-stream I see goroutines sticking around. Any small pattern/snippet people recommend to avoid leaks but keep the API nice for users?
Aj Patel: Yup, this is pretty common. Usually the leak is from a goroutine blocked on sending into a channel or waiting on gen.Next with its own background ctx.
Devon Ross: Which API are you using? Generator.Next(ctx) or a pull-style iterator?
Maria Chen: We expose gen.Next(ctx) that returns (Chunk, error). In our handler we cancel the parent ctx (http request) but the reader goroutine for Next keeps running.
Samira Noor: Pattern that helped me: one goroutine does the blocking Next calls and writes results into a buffered channel. The main loop selects between ctx.Done() and that channel. On cancel, call gen.Close() (or gen.Cancel() if provided), then drain. Snippet below.
Samira Noor: ```go
// reader loop -> out chan
out := make(chan *Chunk, 1)
go func() {
  defer close(out)
  for {
    c, err := gen.Next(context.Background()) // use bg ctx so gen can finish network work
    if err != nil {
      // propagate error by sending a sentinel or set a separate err channel
      return
    }
    // try to send but respect cancel from parent ctx
    select {
    case out <- c:
    case <-parentCtx.Done():
      // actively tell the server we don't need more
      _ = gen.Close() // best-effort close to free server resources
      return
    }
  }
}()

// main loop
for {
  select {
  case <-parentCtx.Done():
    // ensure we close generator and drain so goroutine can exit
    _ = gen.Close()
    for range out { /* drain */ }
    return
  case c, ok := <-out:
    if !ok { return }
    handleChunk(c)
  }
}
```
Samira Noor: Key points: small buffer (1) prevents the reader from blocking on a slow consumer, and using gen.Close() lets the runtime free resources.
Luis Alvarez: Two gotchas I ran into: 1) Some auth/transport retry loops spawn their own goroutines that don't respect the request ctx — make sure your HTTP client/transport supports context cancelation. 2) Make gen.Close() idempotent in the sample so callers can safely call it from multiple places.
Aj Patel: Also if your SDK spawns reconnection loops, have them watch a top-level lifecycle ctx you control per-Generator instance. That way Close can cancel background retries.
Kate Lin: If you want nicer ergonomics, consider providing a helper like ReadChunks(ctx, gen) that returns a channel and handles the reader goroutine + close semantics for the user. Then sampleapps can be one-liners.
CI Bot: note: issue #482 mentions gen.Close race; reproduce case attached in ticket.
Maria Chen: This is awesome — thanks. I'll implement the ReadChunks helper and a minimal sample that demonstrates cancellation + a small test asserting no goroutines after cancel.
Devon Ross: For the sample README add a 'Common pitfalls' section (buffer sizes, auth retries, Close vs Cancel). Short code first, then explanation.
Maria Chen: Will do. I'll ping for reviews once PR is up. Thanks y'all! :pray:
